[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I checked the inter-domain routing “session” (the BGP peering) between the two border routers and it is healthy. On router1 (AS 1) to router2 (AS 2), the BGP neighbor 193.10.11.2/193.10.11.1 is Established with uptime ~2 minutes, keepalive 60s/hold 180s on both sides, and crucially “Connections established 1; dropped 0” on each router. FRR logs on both boxes show only a single transient reset during initial startup, followed by normal UPDATE/KEEPALIVE exchange; there is no evidence of recurring resets. Each router is advertising its LAN (/24) and learning the partner’s; the routes are installed in the kernel FIBs. End-to-end tests from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) show 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs in both directions. Interfaces on both routers are UP, error‑free, and show no queuing drops.

If the BGP session were “resetting itself over and over,” we would observe churn in the neighbor state, incrementing “dropped” counters, and missing routes during the flap window; none of that is present. The routing subsystem (FRR BGP) and the inter-router link (router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0) are behaving correctly.

Root cause: No routing or BGP fault is present; the reported intermittent disconnects are not due to the router-to-router BGP session.

Corrective fix: No changes required on the routers. If the application still experiences drops, investigate endpoint/application timeouts on pc1/pc2 or the partner host. As an optional hardening measure, we can enable BFD or reduce BGP timers to detect real link failures faster, but this is not needed to fix the current report. 
=== END ===